.TH MDDB-CLI 1 "August 2026" "mddb-cli 2.12.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mddb-cli \- command-line client for MDDB (Markdown Database)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mddb-cli
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mddb-cli
is a command-line client for MDDB, providing an interface similar to mysql-client
for managing markdown documents with metadata. It communicates with an MDDB server
via HTTP/JSON API.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-server =\fIURL\fR
MDDB server URL (default: http://localhost:11023)
.TP
.BR \-\-token =\fITOKEN\fR
JWT token for authentication (v2.4+)
.TP
.BR \-\-api-key =\fIKEY\fR
API key for authentication (v2.4+)
.TP
.BR \-\-graphql
Use GraphQL endpoint instead of REST (v2.5+)
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Output raw JSON responses
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help information
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Display version information
.SH COMMANDS
.SS reference
Index other projects' code as reference collections and search them (v2.18.8).
.PP
.B mddb-cli reference add
\fIREPOSITORY\fR [\fB\-\-ref\fR \fIREF\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B mddb-cli reference list
.br
.B mddb-cli reference update
[\fINAME\fR]
.br
.B mddb-cli reference remove
\fINAME\fR
.br
.B mddb-cli reference search
\fIQUERY\fR [\fB\-\-top\-k\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-collection\fR \fINAME\fR]
.PP
add makes a shallow git clone under the user cache directory
(\fBMDDB_REFERENCES_DIR\fR overrides it) and indexes it into
ref-OWNER-REPO with code.role reference, recording the repository, commit and
licence. Only https://, ssh:// and user@host:path addresses are accepted. The
server never clones. search runs a hybrid search over every reference
collection and prints path:lines passages.
.SS eval
Measure retrieval quality on an evaluation set (v2.18.8).
.PP
.B mddb-cli eval
\fICOLLECTION\fR [\fB\-\-queries\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR \fISPEC\fR]... [\fB\-\-k\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-below\fR \fIMETRIC=VALUE,...\fR]
.PP
Runs the collection's stored evaluation set, or the JSON queries in FILE,
under each configuration: fts/ALGORITHM, vector[/INDEX] or hybrid, optionally
followed by BM25 parameters such as :k1=2:b=0.75. It prints
recall@k, nDCG@k, MRR, p50/p99 latency and returned tokens. With
\fB\-\-fail\-below\fR (metrics recall, ndcg, mrr; @N sets k) it exits non-zero
when any configuration scores under a threshold or fails to run.
.SS gain
Show what retrieval saved (v2.18.8).
.PP
.B mddb-cli gain
[\fB\-\-window\fR \fIDAYS\fRd]
.PP
Reports, for the whole server over the window (default 30d, max 365d), the
searches by type and algorithm, the estimated tokens returned, and the tokens
avoided. Tokens avoided are whole documents minus the passages and chunks
returned instead.
.SS alias
Stable names for collections (v2.18.8).
.PP
.B mddb-cli alias list
.br
.B mddb-cli alias set
\fIALIAS\fR \fICOLLECTION\fR
.br
.B mddb-cli alias delete
\fIALIAS\fR
.PP
An alias is accepted wherever a collection name is, on every surface. Moving
it is atomic: a request sees the old collection or the new one.
.SS corpus
Corpus packs: read-only collections built once and installed anywhere (v2.18.10).
.PP
.B mddb-cli corpus build
\fICOLLECTION\fR \fB\-\-license\fR \fILICENSE\fR [\fB\-\-version\fR \fIV\fR]
[\fB\-\-description\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-source\fR \fILICENSE\fR=\fIREPO\fR[@\fICOMMIT\fR]]... [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B mddb-cli corpus install
\fIFILE\fR|\fIhttps://URL\fR [\fB\-\-sha256\fR \fISUM\fR]
.br
.B mddb-cli corpus list
.br
.B mddb-cli corpus remove
\fINAME\fR
.PP
\fBbuild\fR saves a consistent snapshot of the collection \(em documents,
full-text index, vectors, geo points \(em with a manifest naming its sources
and licences; it refuses without \fB\-\-license\fR. \fBinstall\fR uploads a
pack (downloading it first over https when given a URL) and the server
installs it, read-only, under the collection name in its manifest, without a
restart. A pack whose vectors were made by another embedding model is
installed with its vectors disabled: hybrid search on it uses full text only.
\fBremove\fR deletes an installed pack. Writes to an installed pack are
refused with 403. See docs/CORPUS-PACKS.md.
.SS reindex-swap
.B mddb-cli reindex-swap
\fIALIAS\fR \fB\-\-into\fR \fICOLLECTION\fR [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR]
.PP
Copies the documents of the collection ALIAS points at into COLLECTION (its
configuration is copied when it has none), waits until COLLECTION is indexed,
then moves the alias. The old collection is kept.
.SS autoindex
Index every text file under a directory into one collection (v2.18.8).
.PP
.B mddb-cli autoindex
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]
.PP
In a git repository the files are those git tracks or would track; elsewhere
all files except build, dependency and cache directories. Files the server
converts \(em PDF, DOCX, ODT, RTF, XLSX, ODS, PPTX, ODP and EPUB, recognised by
content, and HTML, TeX, CSV, TSV, EML and MBOX, by extension \(em are sent as
files and stored as Markdown (v2.18.9); an email's attachments and a
mailbox's messages become documents of their own. The report lists each
converted document with its format and conversion confidence (0 to 1; a scan
without a text layer scores 0). Other binary files and files over the size
limit are skipped. Each document is keyed by its path and
carries meta path, extension, kind, language, size, modified, and gitCommit
and gitBranch in a repository. Unchanged files are not rewritten. The search
advisor then stores a retrieval profile for the collection.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-collection " " \fINAME\fR
Collection (default: the repository or directory name).
.TP
.BR \-\-lang " " \fICODE\fR
Language stored on every document (default en).
.TP
.BR \-\-include " " \fIGLOB\fR
Only index matching paths; repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude " " \fIGLOB\fR
Skip matching paths; repeatable. dir/** matches a subtree.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-file\-size " " \fIBYTES\fR
Skip larger files (default 1048576).
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " " \fINAME\fR
Ingest profile: fast trades parsing fidelity for speed.
.TP
.B \-\-images
Send each Markdown file with the JPEG, PNG, GIF and WebP files its relative
image links name (inside the indexed tree, at most 2 MB each); the server
stores them, and the pictures of PDFs, as image documents with their alt text
and captions searchable (v2.18.9).
.TP
.B \-\-prune
Delete documents whose file no longer exists.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan \(em files, skipped files and why, documents to prune \(em and write nothing.
.SS add
Add or update a markdown document in the database.
.PP
.B mddb-cli add
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR \fIKEY\fR \fILANG\fR
.PP
Reads content from stdin or a file specified with \fB\-f\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file =\fIFILE\fR
Read content from FILE instead of stdin
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-meta =\fIMETA\fR
Metadata in format: key=val1|val2,key2=val
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
echo "# Hello World" | mddb-cli add blog hello en_US
mddb-cli add blog post1 en_US -f post.md -m "category=tech,author=John"
.fi
.RE
.SS get
Retrieve a document from the database.
.PP
.B mddb-cli get
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR \fIKEY\fR \fILANG\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env =\fIENV\fR
Environment variables for templating: key=val,key2=val2
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-content-only
Output only content (no metadata)
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli get blog hello en_US
mddb-cli get blog post en_US -e "year=2024,author=John"
mddb-cli get blog post en_US -c > output.md
.fi
.RE
.SS search
Search documents in a collection with optional filters.
.PP
.B mddb-cli search
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter =\fIFILTER\fR
Filter by metadata: key=val1|val2,key2=val
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-sort =\fIFIELD\fR
Sort field: addedAt, updatedAt, key (default: updatedAt)
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-asc
Sort ascending (default: descending)
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-limit =\fIN\fR
Limit results (default: 50)
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-offset =\fIN\fR
Offset results (default: 0)
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli search blog
mddb-cli search blog -f "category=tech|tutorial"
mddb-cli search blog -f "category=tech,status=published" -S addedAt -a
mddb-cli search blog -l 10 -o 20
.fi
.RE
.SS export
Export documents from a collection.
.PP
.B mddb-cli export
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-format =\fIFORMAT\fR
Export format: ndjson, zip (default: ndjson)
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output =\fIFILE\fR
Output file (default: stdout)
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter =\fIFILTER\fR
Filter by metadata: key=val1|val2,key2=val
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli export blog -o backup.ndjson
mddb-cli export blog -F zip -o backup.zip
mddb-cli export blog -f "status=published" -o published.ndjson
.fi
.RE
.SS import-url
Import a markdown document from a URL (v2.2+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli import-url
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR \fIURL\fR \fILANG\fR
.PP
Fetches markdown from URL and automatically parses YAML frontmatter as metadata.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key =\fIKEY\fR
Document key (default: derived from URL filename)
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli import-url docs https://example.com/guide.md en_US
mddb-cli import-url kb https://raw.githubusercontent.com/user/repo/README.md en -k readme
.fi
.RE
.SS set-ttl
Set or remove time-to-live (TTL) for a document (v2.0+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli set-ttl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR \fIKEY\fR \fILANG\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-\-ttl =\fISECONDS\fR
TTL in seconds (0 = remove TTL, make permanent)
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
# Expire in 1 hour
mddb-cli set-ttl cache session-123 en --ttl=3600

# Remove TTL (make permanent)
mddb-cli set-ttl cache session-123 en --ttl=0
.fi
.RE
.SS fts
Full-text search using built-in inverted index (v2.2+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli fts
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-\-query =\fIQUERY\fR
Search query (keywords)
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-limit =\fIN\fR
Maximum results (default: 50)
.TP
.B \-\-mode \fImode\fR
Search mode. One of: auto, simple, boolean, phrase, wildcard, proximity (default: auto).
.TP
.B \-\-distance \fIN\fR
Maximum word distance for proximity search (default: 5).
.TP
.B \-\-algorithm \fIalgorithm\fR
FTS scoring algorithm. One of: bm25, bm25f, tfidf, pmisparse (default: bm25).
.TP
.B \-\-fuzzy
Enable fuzzy (typo-tolerant) matching.
.TP
.B \-\-lang \fILANG\fR
Language code for query tokenization (e.g., en, pl, de). Uses language-specific
stemmer and stop words. Falls back to server default if omitted. Supported: en,
pl, de, fr, es, it, pt, nl, ru, sv, no, da, fi, hu, ro, tr, ar, ta (v2.8+).
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli fts blog --query="markdown database tutorial"
mddb-cli fts kb --query="authentication setup" --limit=10
mddb-cli fts articles --query="programowanie wydajne" --lang=pl --algorithm=bm25
.fi
.RE
.SS vector-search
Semantic vector search using embeddings (v2.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli vector-search
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-\-query =\fIQUERY\fR
Search query (natural language)
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-top-k =\fIN\fR
Number of results (default: 5)
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-threshold =\fIFLOAT\fR
Minimum similarity score 0.0-1.0 (default: 0.7)
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-include-content
Include full document content in results
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli vector-search kb --query="how to reset password?"
mddb-cli vector-search blog --query="getting started guide" -k 3 -t 0.8 -c
.fi
.RE
.SS vector-reindex
Re-generate embeddings for all documents in a collection (v2.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli vector-reindex
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-\-force
Force reindex even if embeddings exist
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli vector-reindex kb
mddb-cli vector-reindex blog --force
.fi
.RE
.SS vector-stats
Display vector search statistics (v2.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli vector-stats
.PP
Shows:
.RS
- Total embeddings count
.br
- Embedding dimensions
.br
- Provider and model
.br
- Queue size
.RE
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli vector-stats
mddb-cli vector-stats -j  # JSON output
.fi
.RE
.SS webhook
Manage webhooks for document events (v2.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli webhook
\fISUBCOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.PP
Subcommands:
.TP
.B register
Register a new webhook
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-url =\fIURL\fR
Webhook callback URL (required)
.TP
.BR \-\-events =\fIEVENTS\fR
Comma-separated events: doc.added,doc.updated,doc.deleted
.TP
.BR \-\-collection =\fICOLLECTION\fR
Filter to specific collection (optional)
.RE
.TP
.B list
List all registered webhooks
.TP
.B delete
Delete a webhook by ID
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
# Register webhook for all events
mddb-cli webhook register --url=https://example.com/hook --events=doc.added,doc.updated

# Register for specific collection
mddb-cli webhook register --url=https://cdn.com/purge --events=doc.updated --collection=blog

# List webhooks
mddb-cli webhook list

# Delete webhook
mddb-cli webhook delete abc123
.fi
.RE
.SS schema
Manage JSON Schema validation for collections (v2.3.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli schema
\fISUBCOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.PP
The collection is named with
.BR \-\-collection ,
not positionally.
.PP
Subcommands:
.TP
.B set
Set JSON Schema for a collection
.RS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-collection =\fINAME\fR
Collection name (required)
.TP
.BR \-\-schema =\fIJSON\fR
The schema itself, as a JSON string (required). There is no
.B \-s
shorthand: that is
.B \-\-server
on every command.
.RE
.TP
.B get
Get JSON Schema for a collection
.RS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-collection =\fINAME\fR
Collection name (required)
.RE
.TP
.B delete
Remove JSON Schema from a collection
.RS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-collection =\fINAME\fR
Collection name (required)
.RE
.TP
.B list
List every collection that carries a schema
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
# Set a schema from a file
mddb-cli schema set --collection blog --schema "$(cat blog-schema.json)"

# Get a schema
mddb-cli schema get --collection blog

# Delete a schema
mddb-cli schema delete --collection blog

# List collections with schemas
mddb-cli schema list
.fi
.RE
.SS validate
Validate metadata against a collection's JSON Schema (v2.3.1+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli validate
.BR \-\-collection =\fINAME\fR
.BR \-\-meta =\fIJSON\fR
.PP
Checks a block of metadata, not a stored document, so a schema can be tried
before anything is written.
.B \-\-meta
is JSON here, unlike the
.B key=val1|val2
syntax the document commands use.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli validate --collection blog --meta '{"tag":["go"],"status":"draft"}'
.fi
.RE
.SS backup
Create a backup of the database.
.PP
.B mddb-cli backup
[\fIFILENAME\fR]
.PP
If FILENAME is not specified, generates a timestamped filename.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli backup
mddb-cli backup my-backup.db
.fi
.RE
.SS backup schedule add|list|remove
Scheduled backups (v2.18.9+): cron automations whose action is a backup,
taken by the server and kept to the newest
.B --keep
on a directory inside
.B MDDB_BACKUP_DIR
or in an S3-compatible bucket. The server runs them when
.B MDDB_CRONS=true
is set.
.PP
.B mddb-cli backup schedule add
\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-cron\fR \fIEXPR\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.PP
.B mddb-cli backup schedule list
.PP
.B mddb-cli backup schedule remove
\fIID\fR
.PP
Options for add:
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Schedule name (required).
.TP
.BI \-\-cron " EXPR"
Cron expression, five fields or six with seconds first (required).
.TP
.BI \-\-target " local|s3"
Where backups go (default local).
.TP
.BI \-\-path " DIR"
local: directory inside MDDB_BACKUP_DIR.
.TP
.BI \-\-bucket " NAME"
s3: bucket.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " PREFIX"
Start of every backup's name or key (default mddb-).
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " N"
Backups kept on the target; the oldest are deleted only after a new one is
stored. 0 keeps all.
.TP
.BI \-\-collections " A,B"
Collections to back up (per-collection layout only; default all).
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " HOST[:PORT]" ", \-\-region " REGION ", \-\-access\-key " KEY
s3: connection.
.TP
.BI \-\-secret\-key " KEY"
s3: secret key (default
.BR $MDDB_BACKUP_SECRET_KEY ).
.TP
.B \-\-tls
s3: connect over HTTPS.
.TP
.B \-\-disabled
Create the schedule disabled.
.PP
.B remove
refuses an ID that is not a backup schedule; the backups already taken stay.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli backup schedule add --name nightly --cron "0 3 * * *" --path nightly --keep 7
MDDB_BACKUP_SECRET_KEY=... mddb-cli backup schedule add --name offsite \\
  --cron "0 4 * * *" --target s3 --endpoint s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com --tls \\
  --bucket mddb-backups --prefix prod-eu/ --access-key AKIA... --keep 30
mddb-cli backup schedule list
mddb-cli backup schedule remove 3f9c2a1b7d4e5f60
.fi
.RE

.SS restore
Restore the database from a backup file.
.PP
.B mddb-cli restore
\fIFILENAME\fR
.PP
.B WARNING:
This operation replaces the current database. Use with caution!
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli restore backup-1699296000.db
.fi
.RE
.SS truncate
Remove old revisions from a collection.
.PP
.B mddb-cli truncate
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOLLECTION\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep =\fIN\fR
Number of revisions to keep per document (default: 5)
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-drop-cache
Drop cache (default: true)
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli truncate blog
mddb-cli truncate blog -k 10
mddb-cli truncate blog -k 0  # Remove all revisions
.fi
.RE
.SS stats
Display server and database statistics.
.PP
.B mddb-cli stats
.PP
Shows information about:
.RS
- Database path and size
.br
- Access mode
.br
- Total documents, revisions, and indices
.br
- Per-collection statistics
.RE
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli stats
mddb-cli stats -j  # JSON output
.fi
.RE
.SS login
Authenticate with the MDDB server and obtain a JWT token (v2.4+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli login
\fIUSERNAME\fR \fIPASSWORD\fR
.PP
The JWT token is saved for use in subsequent commands. Use \fB\-\-token\fR flag to override.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli login admin secret
mddb-cli --token YOUR_TOKEN stats  # Use specific token
.fi
.RE
.SS api-key
Manage API keys for programmatic access (v2.4+).
.PP
.B mddb-cli api-key
\fISUBCOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.PP
Subcommands:
.TP
.B create
Create a new API key
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-description =\fIDESC\fR
Description for the API key
.TP
.BR \-\-token =\fITOKEN\fR
JWT token for authentication (required)
.RE
.TP
.B list
List all API keys for current user
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-token =\fITOKEN\fR
JWT token for authentication (required)
.RE
.TP
.B delete
Delete an API key by hash
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-token =\fITOKEN\fR
JWT token for authentication (required)
.RE
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
# Create API key
mddb-cli api-key create --description "Production deployment" --token $TOKEN

# List all keys
mddb-cli api-key list --token $TOKEN

# Delete a key
mddb-cli api-key delete abc123... --token $TOKEN

# Use API key in commands
mddb-cli --api-key mddb_live_xyz789... stats
.fi
.RE
.SS self-update
Update mddb-cli to the latest release.
.PP
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli self-update --check
mddb-cli self-update
.fi
.RE
.PP
The release is fetched from a pinned GitHub URL and its SHA-256 checked against
the release's checksums.txt before anything on disk is touched, so an
interrupted or corrupted download leaves the working binary in place. The
replacement is a rename within the same directory, which is atomic, and the
previous binary is kept as
.IR <path>.bak .
.PP
The checksum proves the download arrived intact. It does not prove the release
is genuine: whoever can publish a release can publish a matching checksum.
Artifact signing would close that and is not configured for this project.
.PP
A binary installed through snap or running inside a container is not replaced —
the packaging channel owns it, and the command names the right one instead.
.PP
.B mddbd
never replaces itself; see
.B mddbd --check-update
for the daemon's read-only equivalent.
.SH GRAPHQL SUPPORT
MDDB provides a GraphQL API alongside the REST API (disabled by default).
.SS Enabling GraphQL on Server
.nf
# Via environment variable
export MDDB_GRAPHQL_ENABLED=true
./mddbd

# Via Docker
docker run -e MDDB_GRAPHQL_ENABLED=true tradik/mddb
.fi
.SS GraphQL Endpoints
.TP
.B /graphql
GraphQL API endpoint (POST requests)
.TP
.B /playground
Interactive GraphQL Playground (development tool)
.SS Example GraphQL Queries
.nf
# Login and get JWT token
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/graphql \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"query": "mutation { login(username: \\"admin\\", password: \\"secret\\") { token } }"}'

# Query with authentication
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/graphql \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"query": "{ me { username admin } }"}'

# Get document
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/graphql \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"query": "{ document(collection: \\"blog\\", key: \\"post\\", lang: \\"en\\") { contentMd } }"}'
.fi
.PP
For full GraphQL documentation, visit:
.I http://localhost:11023/playground
.br
Or see:
.I docs/GRAPHQL.md
.SS graphql
Execute a raw GraphQL query against the MDDB server.
.PP
.B mddb-cli graphql
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIQUERY\fR
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-variables =\fIJSON\fR
GraphQL variables as JSON string
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
# Simple query
mddb-cli graphql '{ stats { totalDocuments } }'

# Mutation
mddb-cli graphql 'mutation { login(username: "admin", password: "secret") { token } }'

# Query with authentication
mddb-cli --token YOUR_JWT_TOKEN graphql '{ me { username admin } }'

# Query with variables
mddb-cli graphql 'query($coll: String!) { search(input: {collection: $coll}) { totalCount } }' \\
  -V '{"coll":"blog"}'

# Get document
mddb-cli graphql 'query { document(collection: "blog", key: "post", lang: "en") { contentMd } }'
.fi
.RE
.SS playground
Open the GraphQL Playground in your default web browser.
.PP
.B mddb-cli playground
.PP
The Playground provides an interactive GraphQL IDE for exploring the schema,
running queries, and viewing documentation.
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
mddb-cli playground
mddb-cli -s http://production.server:11023 playground
.fi
.RE
.SH AGGREGATIONS
MDDB supports metadata aggregations (v2.8+), including facets and date histograms.
.SS Facets
Count distinct values for a metadata key across all documents in a collection.
Useful for building filter sidebars, tag clouds, and category breakdowns.
.SS Histograms
Group documents by time intervals (day, week, month, year) on addedAt or updatedAt fields.
Useful for activity charts and trend analysis.
.SS Example via API
.nf
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/v1/aggregate \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "collection": "blog",
    "facets": [{"field": "category"}, {"field": "author"}],
    "histograms": [{"field": "addedAt", "interval": "month"}]
  }'
.fi
.PP
Supports optional \fBfilterMeta\fR for pre-filtering (same syntax as search).
Also available as MCP tool: \fBaggregate\fR.
.SS Inline Facets on Search (v2.9.15+)
Both \fB/v1/fts\fR and \fB/v1/hybrid-search\fR accept \fBfacetBy\fR (array of
metadata keys) and return a \fBfacets\fR map with per-value counts computed
over the documents that match the query — no separate \fB/v1/aggregate\fR
round-trip. Optional \fBfacetMaxValues\fR caps per-key bucket count.
.nf
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/v1/fts \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"blog","query":"rust","facetBy":["category","lang"],"facetMaxValues":10}'
.fi
.SH CURATION RULES (v2.9.15+)
Curation rules pin specific documents to fixed positions and/or hide others
when a search query matches. Applied inside FTS and Hybrid pipelines after
scoring but before the limit trim; pinned items carry \fBpinned: true\fR on
the response.
.SS Endpoints
.TP
.B GET /v1/curation?id=<id>
Fetch one rule.
.TP
.B GET /v1/curation?collection=<c>
List rules for a collection.
.TP
.B POST /v1/curation
Create a rule (server assigns \fBid\fR).
.TP
.B PUT /v1/curation
Replace an existing rule (body must include \fBid\fR).
.TP
.B DELETE /v1/curation?id=<id>
Remove a rule.
.SS Example
.nf
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/v1/curation \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "collection":"blog",
    "query":"rust tutorial",
    "matchMode":"exact",
    "enabled":true,
    "pins":[{"key":"rust-getting-started","position":1}],
    "hides":["legacy-post"]
  }'
.fi
.PP
\fBmatchMode\fR: \fBexact\fR (default) or \fBcontains\fR. Pins with
\fBposition <= 0\fR are appended after organic results. Also exposed as MCP
tools \fBlist_curation_rules\fR, \fBcreate_curation_rule\fR,
\fBupdate_curation_rule\fR, \fBdelete_curation_rule\fR.
.SH REVISION RETENTION (v2.9.15+)
Per-collection \fBmaxRevisions\fR caps the revision history kept per document.
\fB0\fR (default) = unlimited. \fBN > 0\fR keeps only the newest N revisions;
older entries are dropped inside the same BoltDB transaction as each write,
so high-churn collections can't grow history unbounded.
.nf
curl -X PUT http://localhost:11023/v1/collection-config \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"blog","type":"default","maxRevisions":20}'
.fi
.PP
Also configurable via the Admin Panel's Collection Settings modal, gRPC
\fBSetCollectionConfig\fR, and MCP \fBset_collection_config\fR
(\fBmax_revisions\fR argument).
.SH STORAGE BACKENDS
MDDB supports per-collection storage backends (v2.8+). Each collection can use
a different backend configured via the REST API or the web panel.
.SS Available Backends
.TP
.B boltdb
Default embedded storage. Data is persisted to the BoltDB database file. No
additional configuration required.
.TP
.B memory
In-memory ephemeral storage. All data is lost when the server restarts. Useful
for scratch collections, caching, and testing.
.TP
.B s3
S3-compatible object storage (AWS S3, MinIO, Cloudflare R2, etc.). Requires
\fBstorageConfig\fR with at least \fBendpoint\fR and \fBbucket\fR.
.SS Configuring via API
.nf
# Set a collection to use in-memory storage
curl -X PUT http://localhost:11023/v1/collection-config \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"scratch","storageBackend":"memory"}'

# Set a collection to use S3
curl -X PUT http://localhost:11023/v1/collection-config \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"archive","storageBackend":"s3","storageConfig":{"endpoint":"s3.amazonaws.com","bucket":"my-bucket","useTLS":true}}'
.fi
.PP
Storage backend can also be configured from the web panel under Collection Settings.
.SH VECTOR QUANTIZATION
MDDB supports per-collection vector quantization (v2.9+). Each collection can
store embedding vectors in compressed format to reduce disk and memory usage.
.SS Available Quantization Levels
.TP
.B float32
Default. Full precision, no compression.
.TP
.B int8
4x compression with ~1% recall drop. Recommended for most use cases.
.TP
.B int4
8x compression with ~2-3% recall drop. Best for very large collections.
.SS Configuring via API
.nf
# Enable int8 quantization
curl -X PUT http://localhost:11023/v1/collection-config \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"archive","quantization":"int8"}'

# Reindex to apply quantization to existing vectors
curl -X POST http://localhost:11023/v1/vector-reindex \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"collection":"archive","force":true}'
.fi
.PP
Quantization can also be configured from the web panel under Collection Settings.
For full documentation, see \fBdocs/QUANTIZATION.md\fR.
.SH SERVER-SENT EVENTS
MDDB provides a real-time event stream for document changes (v2.9+).
.SS Endpoint
.TP
.B GET /v1/events
Server-Sent Events (SSE) stream. Broadcasts \fBdoc.added\fR, \fBdoc.updated\fR,
\fBdoc.deleted\fR events with collection, key, lang, and timestamp.
.SS Filtering
Add \fB?collection=NAME\fR to receive events for a specific collection only.
.SS Configuration
Enabled by default. Set \fBMDDB_SSE_ENABLED=false\fR to disable.
Set \fBMDDB_SSE_MAX_CLIENTS=N\fR to limit concurrent connections (default: 1000).
Set \fBMDDB_SSE_MAX_PER_IP=N\fR to limit connections per IP (default: 5).
.PP
When authentication is enabled, SSE requires a valid JWT or API key.
Events are filtered by \fBPermRead\fR per collection. Each event includes
a \fBreadOnly\fR field (\fBtrue\fR unless user has \fBPermWrite\fR).
.SS Example
.nf
# Subscribe to all events
curl -N http://localhost:11023/v1/events

# Subscribe to events for "blog" collection only
curl -N http://localhost:11023/v1/events?collection=blog
.fi
.SH TLS SUPPORT
MDDB supports built-in TLS/HTTPS without a reverse proxy (v2.9+).
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B MDDB_TLS_ENABLED=true
Enable TLS mode.
.TP
.B MDDB_TLS_CERT=/path/to/cert.pem
TLS certificate file (PEM format).
.TP
.B MDDB_TLS_KEY=/path/to/key.pem
TLS private key file (PEM format).
.SS Example
.nf
MDDB_TLS_ENABLED=true \\
MDDB_TLS_CERT=./server.crt \\
MDDB_TLS_KEY=./server.key \\
./mddbd
.fi
.SH MCP-OVER-SSE TRANSPORT
MDDB supports the MCP-over-SSE transport (v2.9.15+) for remote MCP connections
without stdio. Useful for web-based agents and remote servers.
.SS Endpoints (MCP port 9000)
.TP
.B GET /sse
SSE connection. Returns an \fBendpoint\fR event with a URL for posting JSON-RPC.
.TP
.B POST /message?sessionId=X
Send JSON-RPC request to an active SSE session.
.PP
For full documentation, see \fBdocs/LLM_CONNECTIONS.md\fR.
.SH PROFILING
MDDB provides Go pprof profiling endpoints for runtime diagnostics (v2.9+).
Disabled by default. Enable via \fBMDDB_PPROF_ENABLED=true\fR.
.SS Endpoints
.nf
/debug/pprof/          - Index page
/debug/pprof/profile   - CPU profile (?seconds=30)
/debug/pprof/heap      - Heap memory
/debug/pprof/goroutine - Goroutine stacks
/debug/pprof/trace     - Execution trace
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MDDB_SERVER
Default server URL (overridden by \fB\-\-server\fR)
.SH REPLICATION
MDDB supports leader-follower replication allowing horizontal scaling of read operations.
When configuring an MDDB server, you can set the node role using environment variables:

.B MDDB_REPLICATION_ROLE=\fIROLE\fR
  Set to 'leader' or 'follower' (empty means standalone).
  
.B MDDB_REPLICATION_LEADER_ADDR=\fIADDRESS\fR
  For followers, the gRPC address of the leader (e.g., leader:11024).

Followers automatically pull data via binlog streaming over gRPC. You can check the replication status via the HTTP API:
.RS
.nf
curl http://localhost:11023/v1/replication/status
.fi
.RE
For full setup instructions, see \fBdocs/REPLICATION.md\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Workflow
.nf
# Add a document
echo "# My First Post" | mddb-cli add blog first-post en_US \\
  -m "category=blog,author=John Doe"

# Retrieve it
mddb-cli get blog first-post en_US

# Search for all blog posts
mddb-cli search blog -f "category=blog"

# Export all published posts
mddb-cli export blog -f "status=published" -o published.ndjson

# Create a backup
mddb-cli backup daily-backup.db
.fi
.SS Working with Templates
.nf
# Add document with template variables
echo "# Welcome to %%siteName%% in %%year%%" | \\
  mddb-cli add blog welcome en_US

# Retrieve with substitution
mddb-cli get blog welcome en_US -e "siteName=My Blog,year=2024"
.fi
.SS Bulk Operations
.nf
# Import multiple files
for file in posts/*.md; do
  key=$(basename "$file" .md)
  mddb-cli add blog "$key" en_US -f "$file" \\
    -m "category=blog,status=published"
done

# Export and process
mddb-cli export blog -f "category=tutorial" | \\
  jq -r '.key + ": " + .meta.category[0]'
.fi
.SS Maintenance
.nf
# Create daily backup
mddb-cli backup "backup-$(date +%Y-%m-%d).db"

# Clean up old revisions (keep last 5)
mddb-cli truncate blog -k 5

# Restore from backup
mddb-cli restore backup-2024-11-06.db
.fi
.SH METADATA FORMAT
Metadata is specified as comma-separated key=value pairs. Multiple values for
the same key are separated by pipes (|).
.PP
Format: \fBkey1=val1|val2,key2=val3\fR
.PP
This creates:
.RS
.nf
{
  "key1": ["val1", "val2"],
  "key2": ["val3"]
}
.fi
.RE
.SH FILTERING
Filters use the same format as metadata. Multiple values for the same key are
combined with OR logic. Multiple keys are combined with AND logic.
.PP
Example: \fBcategory=tech|tutorial,status=published\fR
.PP
Matches documents where:
.RS
(category = "tech" OR category = "tutorial") AND (status = "published")
.RE
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.SS Human-Readable (default)
Formatted output with labels and structure.
.SS JSON (\-\-json)
Raw JSON responses from the server.
.SS Content-Only (\-c with get command)
Only the markdown content, suitable for piping to other tools.
.SH TIPS
.TP
.B Piping Content
Use \fB\-c\fR with \fBget\fR to pipe content to other tools:
.RS
mddb-cli get blog post en_US -c | pandoc -o post.html
.RE
.TP
.B JSON Processing
Use \fB\-j\fR with \fBjq\fR for advanced processing:
.RS
mddb-cli search blog -j | jq '.[] | select(.meta.category[] == "tech")'
.RE
.TP
.B Batch Operations
Use shell loops for bulk operations:
.RS
for key in post1 post2 post3; do
  mddb-cli get blog "$key" en_US -c > "${key}.md"
done
.RE
.SH AUDIT LOG (v2.9.15+)
When the server is started with
.B MDDB_AUDIT_ENABLED=true
every authentication attempt and every mutating request is recorded to a
dedicated BoltDB bucket. Admin-only endpoint
.B GET /v1/audit
returns the events newest-first. Supported query parameters:
.I from
/
.I to
(RFC3339) or
.I fromNanos
/
.I toNanos
,
.I actor
,
.I action
,
.I result
(\fBok\fR or \fBfail\fR),
.I limit
(default 100).
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_JWT" \e
  "http://localhost:11023/v1/audit?actor=alice&result=fail&limit=50"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Retention is controlled by
.B MDDB_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS
(default 90). Response field
.I dropped
is the running total of events lost to in-memory buffer pressure \(em a
non-zero value is an operational signal to raise the buffer or lower load.
.SH COMPLIANCE CHECK (v2.9.15+)
.B GET /v1/compliance-status
reports the live state of the
.B MDDB_PRODUCTION
hardening guard. Unauthenticated \(em designed for liveness and readiness
probes. Response:
.RS
.nf
{
  "production": true,
  "compliant":  true,
  "missing":    [],
  "missingCount": 0
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
When a guardrail is missing, each entry in
.I missing
carries
.BR envVar ", " want ", " reason .
See
.B /docs/security
for the full ISO 27001 / SOC 2 control map.
.SH RATE LIMITING (v2.9.15+)
With
.B MDDB_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
every HTTP response carries
.BR X-RateLimit-Limit ", " X-RateLimit-Remaining ", " X-RateLimit-Reset .
Rejected requests return
.B 429 Too Many Requests
with a
.B Retry-After
header. The gRPC transport rejects with
.BR codes.ResourceExhausted .
The paths
.IR /health ", " /v1/health ", " /metrics
are exempt so monitoring probes never trip the limiter. The pre-existing
.B MDDB_MCP_RATE_LIMIT_*
budget for MCP is independent and still applies.
.SH ENCRYPTION KEY ROTATION (v2.9.16+)
The 2.9.16 wire format
.B V2
prefixes ciphertext with a 1-byte
.IR keyID
so the encryptor can hold a primary plus any number of read-only previous keys.
.PP
Rotation procedure:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# Capture old key
old_key="$MDDB_ENCRYPTION_KEY"

# Generate new key
export MDDB_ENCRYPTION_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
export MDDB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=2
export MDDB_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_PREVIOUS='[{"id":1,"key":"'$old_key'"}]'

# Restart server, then re-encrypt historical entries
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" \\
  http://localhost:11023/v1/encryption/rotate -d '{}'

# Watch progress
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" \\
  http://localhost:11023/v1/encryption/status
.fi
.RE
.PP
The previous key may be removed from
.B MDDB_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_PREVIOUS
once
.B withLegacy
shows
.B 0
across every collection. Endpoints
.BR /v1/encryption/status ", " /v1/encryption/rotate ", " /v1/encryption/jobs
are admin-only.
.SH AUDIT LOG EXPORT (v2.9.16+)
Mirror every audit event to an external SIEM webhook or syslog collector.
Local BoltDB remains the source of truth — exporters are best-effort.
.TP
.B MDDB_AUDIT_EXPORT_WEBHOOK_URL
Full URL for a SIEM endpoint (Splunk HEC, Datadog Logs, ELK).
.TP
.B MDDB_AUDIT_EXPORT_WEBHOOK_HEADER
Comma-separated headers, e.g.
.RB \(dq Authorization:\ Splunk\ xxx,X-Source:\ prod \(dq.
.TP
.B MDDB_AUDIT_EXPORT_SYSLOG_ADDR
host:port (UDP) or
.BR tcp://host:port .
.TP
.B MDDB_AUDIT_EXPORT_SYSLOG_FACILITY
RFC 5424 facility name (default
.BR local0 ).
.TP
.B GET /v1/audit/exporters
returns delivered/failed/dropped counters per sink.
.SH BACKUP PATH JAIL (v2.9.16+)
.B /v1/backup
and
.B /v1/restore
confine every user-supplied path to
.B MDDB_BACKUP_DIR
(default
.IR ./backups ).
Absolute paths,
.IR \(dq..\(dq ,
NUL bytes, and symlinks that escape the jail are rejected with
.B InvalidArgument
on gRPC and 400 on HTTP.
